{"notes": "Labour tightness is easing at the margin. The unemployment rate has drifted to 3.5%, down 10bp in three months but up 10bp from the 12-month low—a rate now roughly 50bp below the natural rate estimate. The Sahm gap at +10bp signals early recession risk but remains mild. The most recent observation sits at the bottom of the 12-month range with minimal room to compress further, and the 3-month average of 3.60% shows gentle upward drift. This reads as a labour market that has cooled from spring levels but retains measurable slack relative to its longer-term floor. The trajectory is loosening, not tightening.

